    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What relationships or patterns do you notice in the coordinates? Be prepared to share what you notice with the class.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Other than \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\), \\((\\text-1,0)\\), and \\((0,\\text-1)\\), the coordinates we used in this activity involved approximations. The point \\((0.8,0.6)\\), however, lies exactly on our unit circle.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Explain why this must be true.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Find all other points on the unit circle that also lie exactly at the intersection of two grid lines.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What are the approximate angle measures needed to intersect at \\((0.8, 0.6)\\) and each of these new points?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Given any point in a quadrant on the unit circle and its associated angle, like \\(R\\) shown here, we can make some statements about other points that must also be on the unit circle.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For example, if the coordinates of \\(R\\) are \\((\\text-0.87,0.5)\\) and \\(a\\) is \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\) radians, then there is a point \\(S\\) in quadrant 1 with coordinates \\((0.87,0.5)\\). Since \\(R\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\) radians from a half…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is the matching point to \\(R\\) in quadrant 4? (A point at \\((0.87,\\text-0.5)\\) and angle \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}\\) radians.)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In future lessons, we’ll learn about how to find the coordinates of point \\(R\\) ourselves using its angle \\(a\\) and what we know about right triangles.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The length of an interval at which a periodic function repeats. A function \\(f\\) has a period, \\(p\\), if \\(f(x+p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L04-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Angle Coordinates Galore",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function whose values repeat at regular intervals. If \\(f\\) is a periodic function then there is a number \\(p\\), called the period, so that \\(f(x + p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
